\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Curl and the Hodge star}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{Vector calculus writes #{\nabla\times a}, and differential forms
write #{\star\mathrm d a}. Geometric calculus writes
#{-{\psI}(\covwedge a)}. In #{\operatorname{Cl}(3,0)},
##{\nabla\times a=-{\psI}(\covwedge a).}
The Hodge dual is the product with #{\psI^{-1}}, with the sign in the
discussion above. The pseudoscalar #{\psI} is introduced with the
Clifford product in a later part; the two marks #{\psI} and #{\bbI}
stay distinct.}
